WEBVTT

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:28.730
<b>LEGEND</b>

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:28.730
<b>OF THE</b>

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:28.730
<b>  GALACTIC HEROES</b>

00:01:52.890 --> 00:02:02.510
Chapter III

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.500
Search: Admiral Bruce
Ashbey's biographies.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.750
There are 123
books available.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.510
In addition, there are
12 TV dramas and movies.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:18.660
Which one would you like ?

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.610
Oh, well.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.490
In the history of the Free Planets
Alliance Fleet Bruce Ashbey...

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.880
...was a very prominent person famed for his many accomplishments

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.800
He was born in Space Year 710, and
when he died in 745, he was an Admiral.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.240
After his death, he was
promoted to Fleet Admiral.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.150
He was a distinguished commander who led the Alliance to a decisive victory in the 2nd Battle of Tiamat.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:50.470
Only Limpao and Yusuf Tparl at the Battle of Dagon could match his skill on the battlefield.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:54.640
Well, he's way up there compared
to an instant hero like Yang Wenli.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.350
Needless to say, he was the valedictorian
at the military academy upon graduation...

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.310
...and he was said to be the best
student since the school's founding.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.150
When he fell to second place in his third
year, he was said to be so mortified...

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.080
...as to claim that it was
the worst dishonor of his life.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.050
In my case, I was always relieved when
I avoided flunking a course every year.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.870
At the age of 20, Bruce
Ashbey's height was 186 cm{6'1"}.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:21.140
With his tall, well-proportioned frame and youthful, ambitious eyes, he was a clean-cut handsome man.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.730
He was said to possess the distinguished
appearance of a leader who stands above the crowd.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.510
When he was an Ensign, it was said that he
looked more dignified than any Lt. Commander.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.180
That's an exaggeration.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.440
But Bruce Ashbey certainly had a dignity that allowed him to lead the Fleet while he was still young.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.510
His sharp eyes could intimidate people, but his
slightly smiling mouth softened that impression.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.770
Warmth and dignity...

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.310
...he possessed the
qualities of a true leader.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:56.570
Compared to that, the instant hero, Yang
Wenli, doesn't have the gallant figure...

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.200
...or dignified air of someone
who stands above others.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.610
But I'm not trying to
be like Bruce Ashbey.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.590
Bruce Ashbey's headquarters staff was
comprised of his former classmates.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:16.200
Since they graduated in June 730,
they were called the "Year 730 Mafia"...

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.210
...but it doesn't mean they
were particularly exclusive.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:23.820
However, it is a historical rarity for classmates to form the core of a military force...

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.550
...and garner exceptional acclaim
over the span of 15 years...

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.300
...and for that reason,
their existence stands out.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.350
The "Year 730 Mafia"...

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.200
Besides their leader, Bruce Ashbey, the others were Alfred Rosas, Frederick Jasper...

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.840
...Wallice Warrick, Vittorio di Bertini,
Fang Tchewling, and John Drinker Cope.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.490
All of them were
promoted to Admiral.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:54.190
At the present time, Space Year 788,
only Alfred Rosas is still alive.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.000
That means he's 78 years old.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.060
In both action and results,
Ashbey had a flair for the dramatic.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.240
And if we were to use a theater metaphor,
he was more like a big star than a good actor.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:10.570
The battles he participated in were famous
battles solely because he participated.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.110
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:16.650
First, there was Bruce Ashbey,
and then there were the battles.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:22.500
This statement may invite the false impression
that Ashbey was a ferocious battle maniac.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.740
He was an Admiral
at the age of 35.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.470
He was a purely professional military officer
who served as Space Warfare Commander...

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:34.200
...of the Free Planets Alliance Fleet, and he
didn't have any doubt about fighting the war.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.220
But there is no evidence that, as a person,
he had a morally degenerate character.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:46.210
Ashbey, as if to exercise his rights, was active and assertive when he fulfilled his military obligations.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:52.010
That was the statement from his trusted
command staff officer, Admiral Alfred Rosas.

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:55.960
His memoir, which was published
in 778, won a nonfiction prize...

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.220
...and is considered to be one of the most
important documents in the Alliance Fleet.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.950
Admiral Rosas's memoir...

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.460
Naturally, Bruce Ashbey's name was
not unknown to the Imperial Forces.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:15.900
In Space Year 740, which is Imperial Year 431, there was a mention of Bruce Ashbey as...

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.290
..."the rebel's big star" in the public
documents of the Empire's Military Ministry.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.780
At this point, Ashbey's presence was already something the enemy couldn't ignore.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.430
That was, of course, due to his
accomplishments, but at the same time...

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.130
...it can't be denied that it was
also partly due to propaganda.

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:36.510
As is often the case for military heroes,
Bruce Ashbey also had audacity and panache.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:42.230
Each time he won a battle he sent messages to the defeated opposition and gloated over his victories.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:49.250
In Space Year 742, there is a record of 
his message after the Battle of Dragonia.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.800
This is a message
for the Imperial Forces.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.960
The person who beat
you was Bruce Ashbey.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.820
The person who will beat you next
time will also be Bruce Ashbey.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:00.900
Don't you dare forget that.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.790
That must have enraged
the Empire, I'm sure.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:09.490
Computer, are there any documents from the
Imperial Forces concerning Bruce Ashbey ?

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:13.330
The data bank does not have official
records from the Empire available.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:17.700
Among the documents from Phezzan there are
files that appear to be based on Imperial records.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:18.980
Send me those.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.200
This is a film dated from
Imperial Caldendar 433...

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.940
...of the Chief Commander of the Military
Ministry, Fleet Admiral Keltring...

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.680
...when he learned
about Ashbey's doings.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.200
I don't care if he's dead or alive -
just get Bruce Ashbey.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.820
Whoever does that will be given all the
wealth and fame possible and imaginable !

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.680
The Fleet Admiral's oldest son, Herman, and second son, Karl Heintz both died young...

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.200
...in battles with
Ashbey's forces.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.230
The Admiral's hatred of Ashbey
was both official and personal.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.700
The Fleet Admiral's 
speech excited the soldiers.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:57.550
But when desire matches the result, that is a moment of utmost happiness in life...

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.200
...and the Fleet Admiral was not
rewarded with this happiness.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.670
Before the 2nd Battle of Tiamat,
Fleet Admiral Keltring fell ill...

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.720
...while he was still in active
service with the Military Ministry.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.150
When his nephew, Wilhelm von Muckenberger, paid him a visit to wish him well...

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:16.930
...he held his nephew's hand tightly and died after twice telling him to kill Ashbey in a dying voice.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.550
Shall I call it blind vengeance ?
It's a dramatic story.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.800
But it does feel like Ashbey fueled the
Empire's anger much more than necessary.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.330
Of course, this anecdote is
well known in the Alliance.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.500
But what actually happened ?

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.270
The Empire's information
is available via Phezzan...

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:40.390
...but I can't know how the information might be doctored while in transit.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:41.500
I can't assume anything.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.290
 What was Admiral Bruce
Ashbey like in reality ?

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:50.200
In the Alliance, the date of his death in battle is now commemorated as a national holiday.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:54.650
He is admired as a national hero, and we
never hear anything unfavorable about him.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.200
But since he was also human,
he must have had some shortcomings.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:01.000
To find out about those wouldn't mean libel against him, but on the other hand...

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.340
...it is also certain that there is a tacit
agreement that we can't say anything{unfavorable about Ashbey}.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:13.460
Still, it isn't fair to ignore his merits and exaggerate
his shortcomings for the purpose of refuting legend.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:20.930
Is it because of his childish vanity that he
actively encouraged the Empire's hatred ?

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:25.610
No, might it have been a type
of psychological warfare ?

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.700
Anger and hatred
overwhelmed saner reason...

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.080
...and the Empire's attention
focused solely on Ashbey.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.550
As a result, their battles lost sight of the larger strategic picture and resulted...

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.630
...in defeats at the hand of Ashbey's
graceful individual tactics time after time.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.220
If that's the case, it's a
noteworthy script writing job.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:49.870
He knew how to maximize
his talents, I suppose.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.250
Hey, how's your
project going ?

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.230
I just got started.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.220
I'm still reviewing
the textbook material.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:05.340
Yeah, the basics are important.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:06.700
By the way, why
did you call me ?

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:12.400
I forgot to ask this yesterday, but wasn't
there a signature on those letters ?

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.510
There was, wasn't there ?

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.100
What is it ?

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.750
All right...
There was a signature.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.550
The signature purported to
be that of "Lucinda Ashbey."

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:30.570
Lucinda Ashbey ?

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:34.090
She was the second
wife of Admiral Ashbey.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:35.720
She's a widow ?

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.100
No, they divorced before
the Admiral's death...

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.750
...so she does not exactly have
the right to use Ashbey's name.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.710
But even after the divorce, she used
Ashbey's name, and because of it...

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.720
...her relationship with the Admiral
was somewhat acrimonious.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:56.010
And she's the one who is questioning
Ashbey's death, am I correct ?

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.280
But Lucinda died
nine years ago.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:02.530
At the time of
death, she was 59.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:06.600
The cause of death is recorded as an
accidental overdose of sleeping pills.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.790
Unless it's in vogue in heaven
to send letters down here...

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:14.490
...that means someone alive
is appropriating her name.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:18.760
If they did their research,
they'd have known she's dead.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.980
Either they didn't
do their homework, or...

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.700
They knew, but still intentionally
used a dead person's name.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:28.630
How's it going ?

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.860
This is piquing your
interest a little bit, isn't it ?

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:32.950
Y...yes, but...

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.990
I'm not quite sure how
to go about this project.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.520
Should I take it seriously, or is it
just something to kill time with ?

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:46.700
No, but whether it's Commander
Cazellnu or someone at Headquarters...

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:50.130
...with what intentions did
they give me this assignment ?

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.800
What's the matter ?

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:52.900
Nothing.

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.740
So, is there anything else
that you're hiding from me ?

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.730
That sounds malicious.
I'm not hiding anything.

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:03.930
As for the matter with Mrs. Ashbey, I didn't
mention it because it was too preposterous.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:05.240
Are you sure ?

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.520
That's the way, just
try doubting everything.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:10.250
Did you want
anything else ?

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.150
About Mrs. Ashbey, could you give
me a little more in-depth information ?

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:20.060
Bruce Ashbey married twice,
when he was 23 and 29.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:24.060
And then he divorced twice,
when he was 26 and 30.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:28.760
He had known his first wife, Adelaide,
since his military academy days...

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:33.870
...and she was also a mutual
friend of the Year 730 Mafia.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.870
The second wife is the
aforementioned Lucinda.

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:39.850
The reason for the divorces appears to be
Admiral Ashbey's habitual extramarital affairs.

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:41.890
Habitual extramarital affairs ?

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:43.580
It wasn't just
when he was single.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:50.340
Even when he was married, his lovers and
secret lovers numbered more than a squadron.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:51.440
That many ?

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.550
He must have
been a busy man.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:56.380
Whether it's Fleet Admiral Limpao
or Fleet Admiral Ashbey...

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.040
...the heroes of this nation
tend to be men of many loves.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:04.100
Turning the tables, how about
our dearly beloved Yang Wenli ?

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:13.650
Well, I'd like to be popular too if I could, but
I'm not motivated enough to make the effort.

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:17.180
If I had that kind of time or energy,
I'd rather spend it reading books.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.060
Oh, well.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:19.050
How lazy.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:26.800
But the other paired with Limpao, Yusuf
Tparl, was famous for hating women...

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.330
...so amorousness is not necessarily
a requirement for a hero.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:35.170
Certainly, Fleet Admiral Tparl never married,
but that doesn't mean he didn't have any lovers.

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:38.050
There are records
of letter exchanges.

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.150
Wow, Fleet Admiral
Tparl's love letters !

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:42.390
I didn't know that.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.800
I don't know if you can
call them "love letters."

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:45.860
What do you mean ?

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.230
"Influenza is spreading."

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:53.380
"Since there have been 1,000,000 patients,
try not to be patient number 1,000,001."

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.550
Or "I'm an insensitive man..."

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:57.630
"...so it'll inconvenience me if you expect
me to be sensitive to your feelings."

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:01.370
"If you have any complaints,
you must speak up."

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:02.570
Things like that.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:07.030
Rather than love or romance, it
sounds more like a "goal of the week."

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.190
That's more like
Fleet Admiral Tparl.

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.800
Yes, I guess it all
depends on who you are.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:18.500
Limpao and his fierceness,
Yusuf Tparl's stringent strictness...

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:20.000
...Bruce Ashbey's gracefulness...

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.200
...we could call those the reasons for their
appeal or, perhaps shortcomings as well.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:27.780
But I wouldn't measure
up to any one of those.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:33.650
Getting back to your project, Fleet
Admiral Ashbey's first wife is still alive.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:38.530
But she cut all contact with
Ashbey after their divorce...

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.920
...at least on the surface.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:43.200
If you want to meet
her, we can arrange it.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:47.240
If there's a need, I will ask for your help,
but I think she's probably uninvolved.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:53.160
If someone you parted with marries 
someone else, you might still retain interest...

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:58.340
...but it might not be strong enough to
actually to take some kind of action.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:03.740
I see.

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.160
Thank you for
your trouble.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:08.080
If you need anything,
just let me know.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:21.100
Give me the files of the members of the
Year 730 Mafia other than Bruce Ashbey.

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:23.520
The excerpts from Admiral
Rosas's memoir will do.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:30.190
Frederick Jasper was fearless and intelligent,
with a straight-shooter personality.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:34.430
He was called "March Jasper"
for his dynamic tactical proclivity.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:39.450
He was always flashy,
in winning or in losing...

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:46.200
...and a KO was his
only end, either way.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.130
"Half-hearted is not my
style" was his motto.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:52.330
In his dictionary, there was 
"complete victory" but no "close victory...

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:56.050
...and there was "total debacle"
but no "close defeat."

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.100
He had a strange jinx.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.060
When he won twice, he
always lost the next battle.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:10.200
His crew also knew of this trend of victory, victory, loss, victory, victory, loss...

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.550
...and when they had to fight in the "loss" battle, they wrote their wills.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:18.450
It's not a laughing matter, as some soldiers tried to escape on those "loss" turns.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:24.200
But this tanned, dark-haired man
was well liked by his soldiers.

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:28.200
Also known as "Baron",
Wallice Warwick...

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.900
...was, of course, a citizen of the
Alliance and not an aristocrat.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:39.150
He was called "Baron" only because his appearance and behavior were somewhat affected and theatrical.

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:43.650
That nickname had a satirical meaning of "no matter how much he tries, he can't be a Count or Earl."

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:47.500
But despite knowing it, he
prided himself on the nickname...

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:54.700
...and always added it to his name
when he had to introduce himself.

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:59.050
Though Warwick may not
have been a genius...

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:05.480
...he was a competent commander
and indispensable to Ashbey.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:11.350
With his soft blond hair, his eyes of
the same color, and medium height...

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:16.710
...he was well liked by women,
and he too liked women very much.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:20.000
Especially beautiful young women
with well-proportioned teeth.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:27.840
Outside the military, he had many talents,
such as tarot card reading and dancing.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:33.520
He played the guitar and trumpet,
played chess, darts, skied...

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:37.640
...and of course had many loves,
providing his life with many adventures.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:42.400
In everything he did, he could
become one step short of a master.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:47.630
In the commentaries of Admiral Rosas,
there's a slightly bitter but definite affection.

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:51.200
He might have been regretful for the friend
who accepted his many faceted talents...

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.820
...but lacked the persistence to
become a true master in one field.

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.190
I don't mind
being under Ashbey.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:00.140
I'm not suited for being
the top commander.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:05.900
I'd always like, let's see,
to be a skilled amateur.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:09.240
Was Warwick concealing
his true self ?

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:15.700
He was a professional military officer, but
perhaps because he was a professional...

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:19.920
...he was swallowing the bitter truth
with a medicinal wafer of "humor."

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:23.140
When he graduated from the
military academy, he was 7th.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.670
Ashbey was always
ahead of him.

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:29.800
In order to deal with his complex feelings,
perhaps he needed some humor.

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.200
John Drinker Cope's
middle name...

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:38.490
...is not a nickname for a drunkard,
but a legitimate, real name.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:44.450
The name is somewhat ironic...

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:50.220
He was allergic to alcohol and
couldn't drink even one sip.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.310
Even his victory
drink was apple juice.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:58.240
One time, when Baron Warwick played
a joke and substituted champagne...

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:03.310
...he developed hives immediately after
he drank it, causing a medical incident.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:06.200
Warwick, though it
was his own fault...

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:11.550
...became the first admiral in Alliance
Force history to file a report for hives.

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:16.600
Cope was also a
competent strategist.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:19.550
He understood and executed
his given strategies well...

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:22.130
...and contributed to the
Alliance Force's victories.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:26.500
He was especially skilled at pursuing the
escaping enemy and shaving their numbers.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:34.200
Vittorio di Bertini was generally known as a
coarse, front-line lower rank officer type...

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:38.200
...but he led battles
bravely and was dedicated.

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:41.220
His destructive capability
often eclipsed that of Ashbey.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:48.130
He looked like a heavy weight boxer,
and with facial scars and tanned skin...

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:54.950
...his appearance reinforced his image as a rough and ferocious commander.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:59.390
But he was soft hearted
in everyday life.

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:04.000
He married a woman half his
height, and he just smiled...

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:09.250
...when Jasper ridiculed it as
"the marriage of a bear and squirrel."

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.200
He also liked tropical fish.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:15.750
There are rumors that he named his tropical fish after his battle friends...{i.e. 730 Mafia}

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.630
...but the veracity of that
has not been confirmed.

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:28.410
Fang Tchewling was of true Asian descent, and so "Fang" was his surname.

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.460
His tactical capabilities did not
seem to be that of a genius...

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:33.200
...but they were
very dependable...

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:37.060
...and were based on meticulous
calculations and perfectionist preparations.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:42.300
Never one to collapse, he often maintained
his position in otherwise losing battles...

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:46.980
...and often created opportunities to reverse the outcomes of battles.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.200
He was a difficult
and serious man...

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:52.800
...and even when someone cracked
a joke and everyone was laughing...

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:54.200
...he did not even break
out the hint of smile.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:59.220
One time, out of blue, he shared
a humorous story with his friends.

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:03.750
After he told the story
and everyone laughed...

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:09.800
...he asked his friends what
was so funny about the story.

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:11.800
His friends didn't
know what to say.

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:18.200
He didn't know how to give lip service, either to his superiors or to his juniors, so he was not very popular.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:23.550
Ashbey may have trusted this
dependable man more than anyone else...

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.340
...though it doesn't
mean he liked him.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.530
And now, myself, Alfred Rosas.

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:33.370
I was not blessed with
tremendous talent like Ashbey.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:37.200
My job was to
listen to everyone...

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.550
...and coordinate their opinions
in order to serve as a buffer...

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:43.990
...among the strong personalities
at General Headquarters.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:50.860
In order to make use of these vastly different talents, a living "glue" was a necessity.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:56.800
I saw my raison-d'etre in fulfilling that need, and actively put myself in that role.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:00.470
And in actuality, when I was
a commanding officer on my own...

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:04.500
...I would think that my competence was
probably only slightly above average.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:08.900
But having served as Ashbey's chief of staff
six times over the course of ten years...

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:12.640
...I believe I had his
confidence and trust.

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:15.200
In Space Year 745...

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.760
...Ashbey was appointed Chief Commander
of the Space Fleet, and in June of that year...

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:23.350
...I was appointed to be the Chief
of General Staff of the Space Fleet.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:28.490
Though there were whispers of resentment
over these 730 Mafia-centric assignments...

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:31.060
...our achievements are
matters of public knowledge.

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.250
These are from his memoirs.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.550
He's the only one still
alive among the 730 Mafia.

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:44.630
So I think I should first meet with
him and listen to what he has to say.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:56.560
Thus, Yang Wenli was to pay a visit
to Admiral Alfred Rosas's residence.

00:22:56.560 --> 00:23:01.280
This happened on October 4th,
Space Year 788.

